          "authorFinding": {
            "id": "platform-parity-001",
            "title": "Windows and macOS data_dir return a hardcoded Linux path",
            "lens": "platform-parity",
            "anchor": {
              "path": "reasoning-platform-parity/planted/src-tauri/path.rs",
              "line": 3,
              "text": "pub fn data_dir() -> PathBuf { PathBuf::from(\"/home/app/.local/share/app\") }"
            },
            "severity": "high",
            "confidence": "confirmed",
            "disposition": "wrong",
            "evidence": [
              "reasoning-platform-parity/planted/src-tauri/path.rs:3",
              "reasoning-platform-parity/planted/src-tauri/path.rs:5",
              "reasoning-platform-parity/planted/src-tauri/path.rs:7"
            ],
            "sourceLocation": "reasoning-platform-parity/planted/src-tauri/path.rs:3",
            "sourceQuote": "#[cfg(target_os = \"windows\")]\npub fn data_dir() -> PathBuf { PathBuf::from(\"/home/app/.local/share/app\") }",
            "failureScenario": "The windows and macos cfg branches return the same literal Linux path, /home/app/.local/share/app, as the linux branch. On Windows this is a drive-relative path that doesn't resolve to a per-user data directory such as %LOCALAPPDATA%. On macOS it points at /home, which is an automount point and is normally not writable. Any caller that creates or opens files under data_dir() will fail or write to the wrong place. The linux branch also ignores XDG_DATA_HOME and HOME, so it breaks for any user whose home isn't /home/app. The clean sibling resolves each OS through LOCALAPPDATA, HOME/Library/Application Support, and XDG_DATA_HOME or HOME/.local/share, and returns io::Result so a missing environment variable is reported. The planted version drops that error path.",
            "action": "Replace the per-OS bodies with real resolution, as in the clean version (path.rs lines 3-13). Windows should use LOCALAPPDATA. macOS should use HOME/Library/Application Support/app. Linux should use an absolute XDG_DATA_HOME, falling back to HOME/.local/share/app. Keep the io::Result signature so a missing environment variable is reported. Add per-OS CI coverage that runs data_dir() on Windows, macOS and Linux.",
            "verifyStatus": "not-applicable"
          },
